WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.260 --> 00:00:04.500
We'll probably never know the reasons for
the death of every character who's died on

00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:08.800
a TV show, but some stories from the behind
the scenes just couldn't stay under wraps.

00:00:08.800 --> 00:00:12.680
Here are some of the most scandalous reasons
some well-known TV characters kicked the fictional

00:00:12.680 --> 00:00:13.680
bucket.

00:00:13.980 --> 00:00:18.840
Shannen Doherty is arguably better known for
being difficult to work with than she is for

00:00:18.840 --> 00:00:22.360
her acting. After Tori Spelling witnessed
what looked like a fistfight between her and

00:00:22.360 --> 00:00:26.560
Jennie Garth on the set of 90201, she asked
her father, producer Aaron Spelling, to fire

00:00:26.560 --> 00:00:27.760
Doherty.

00:00:28.140 --> 00:00:33.800
Just a few years later, however, Aaron Spelling
surprisingly cast Doherty in another project,

00:00:33.809 --> 00:00:39.750
the WB supernatural drama Charmed. History
repeated itself: Doherty quit the show in

00:00:39.750 --> 00:00:44.300
2001 after reports of a feud with co-star
Alyssa Milano. After a season three cliffhanger

00:00:44.300 --> 00:00:48.670
that left her character's fate in jeopardy,
the season four opener revealed that the character

00:00:48.670 --> 00:00:49.380
had died.

00:00:49.720 --> 00:00:54.800
"I don't understand why magic can't fix this,
and why it can't bring Prue back. It's not

00:00:54.820 --> 00:00:59.240
like we haven't cheated death before. I don't
understand why this time is any different."

00:00:59.780 --> 00:01:05.060
T. R. Knight left Grey's Anatomy in 2009 after
five seasons despite the fact that his character,

00:01:05.070 --> 00:01:09.300
young doctor George O'Malley, was a fan favorite.
Knight knew something was up with the show's

00:01:09.300 --> 00:01:15.100
first nine episodes of the 2008-09 season
when he landed only 48 minutes of screen time,

00:01:15.100 --> 00:01:19.660
less than half of co-star Sandra Oh. Rather
than ask producer Shonda Rhimes just what

00:01:19.660 --> 00:01:23.920
was happening, he walked away instead. He
told Entertainment Weekly,

00:01:31.440 --> 00:01:36.320
Perhaps he angered Rhimes. His horrific death
could certainly imply that: He's hit by a

00:01:36.320 --> 00:01:40.870
bus, admitted to the very hospital where he
worked, and, after his doctor friends finally

00:01:40.870 --> 00:01:44.440
recognize him, he's pronounced brain-dead
and passes away.

00:01:45.160 --> 00:01:50.840
One of the biggest new shows of the 2004-05
season was also among the most innovative:

00:01:50.840 --> 00:01:55.950
ABC's time- and mind-bending desert island
sci-fi adventure series Lost. Many of its

00:01:55.950 --> 00:02:00.710
cast became stars, and the show won the Emmy
for Outstanding Drama Series. But some actors

00:02:00.710 --> 00:02:05.700
on the show didn't enjoy the process of making
the series. Adewale Akinnuoye-Agbaje, who

00:02:05.700 --> 00:02:10.531
played the mysterious and conflicted Nigerian
priest Mr. Eko, left the series in Season

00:02:10.531 --> 00:02:16.330
3. Why? Well, while this might be hard to
believe, Akinnuoye-Agbaje hated filming in

00:02:16.330 --> 00:02:21.370
Hawaii. He asked to leave the show, and long-term
plans for Mr. Eko were cancelled. The character

00:02:21.370 --> 00:02:24.040
was killed off, a victim of the Smoke Monster

00:02:24.440 --> 00:02:31.180
"The Lord is my shepherd, I shall not want.
The Lord is my shepherd…"

00:02:34.400 --> 00:02:39.219
Eko wasn't the only character killed off the
beloved sci-fi show. From the outside, it

00:02:39.219 --> 00:02:43.900
appeared that Lost characters Ana Lucia Cortez
and Libby Smith, portrayed by Michelle Rodriguez

00:02:43.900 --> 00:02:48.680
and Cynthia Watros, met their ends due to
offscreen misbehavior. During the middle of

00:02:48.680 --> 00:02:53.150
the show's second season, both actresses were
arrested on suspicion of drunk driving and

00:02:53.150 --> 00:02:56.820
failed sobriety tests. Not long after, both
characters died.

00:02:56.820 --> 00:03:02.099
In actuality, the arrests didn't affect the
show at all. In an interview with TV Guide,

00:03:02.100 --> 00:03:06.240
Lost co-creator Damon Lindelof said that the
characters' deaths had already been written.

00:03:06.240 --> 00:03:10.700
In the case of Ana Lucia Cortez, Michelle
Rodriguez only wanted to do one season of

00:03:10.700 --> 00:03:15.030
the show anyhow. The fact that her character
also happened to die at the same time as Watros'

00:03:15.030 --> 00:03:16.920
Libby was just a coincidence.

00:03:17.520 --> 00:03:21.660
Seinfeld's occasional callous cruelty was
part of the appeal of the show, but the show

00:03:21.660 --> 00:03:26.579
outdid itself in one episode when it killed
off a major recurring character. George Costanza's

00:03:26.579 --> 00:03:31.879
fiancée, Susan Ross, died at the end of the
seventh season, having ingested a toxic amount

00:03:31.880 --> 00:03:34.240
of glue from licking wedding invitation envelopes.

00:03:34.340 --> 00:03:37.520
"So, she's dead."

00:03:40.080 --> 00:03:41.240
"Yes."

00:03:43.160 --> 00:03:43.940
"Huh."

00:03:44.180 --> 00:03:48.580
Apparently, George, who'd wanted out of the
engagement, wasn't the only one happy to hear

00:03:48.590 --> 00:03:52.951
the news of the character's death. In an interview
with Howard Stern several years later, actor

00:03:52.960 --> 00:03:57.460
Jason Alexander revealed that actress Heidi
Swedberg just didn't sync up with him:

00:03:57.700 --> 00:04:04.380
"Her instincts for doing a scene, where the
comedy was, and mine were always misfiring."

00:04:04.580 --> 00:04:09.080
He wasn't the only one on set who felt that
way. Alexander related how his co-star Julia

00:04:09.080 --> 00:04:12.060
Louis-Dreyfus agreed with him, casually remarking:

00:04:13.980 --> 00:04:18.140
Once writer Larry David heard this, that was
the end of Susan Ross.

00:04:18.680 --> 00:04:23.540
Poor Carla Tortelli. Just when it looked like
she'd finally found true love with hockey

00:04:23.540 --> 00:04:28.670
player Eddie LeBec, the poor doofus went and
got himself killed. While Cheers was generally

00:04:28.670 --> 00:04:33.060
one of the more reserved comedies on television
during its 11-year run, the writers scratched

00:04:33.060 --> 00:04:38.110
their wacky itch when it came time to kill
Eddie. He's killed in a bizarre Zamboni accident,

00:04:38.110 --> 00:04:41.910
setting up a wacky chain of events that led
to Carla meeting Eddie's secret other wife

00:04:41.910 --> 00:04:47.400
during the funeral. In 2006, Cheers writer
Ken Levine revealed on his blog just why they

00:04:47.400 --> 00:04:52.330
killed off Eddie LeBec, and in such a perplexing
way. Actor Jay Thomas, who also worked as

00:04:52.330 --> 00:04:57.120
a radio DJ, responded to a caller who asked
him how it felt to act on Cheers. Levine recalled

00:04:57.120 --> 00:05:00.120
Thomas making some distasteful remarks about
his co-star.

00:05:03.400 --> 00:05:08.790
That got him fired, and it got Levine and
writing partner David Isaacs to write Eddie

00:05:08.790 --> 00:05:10.000
LeBec's bizarre death.

00:05:10.820 --> 00:05:15.640
While House had its lighter moments, it could
get pretty dark too. One example: the fate

00:05:15.650 --> 00:05:19.980
of eager diagnostic medicine fellow Lawrence
Kutner. The character had to make a hasty

00:05:19.980 --> 00:05:25.060
exit when actor Kal Penn embarked on a political
career partway through the show's run in 2009,

00:05:25.060 --> 00:05:29.750
taking a job with the brand-new Obama administration.
Not wanting to pass up the opportunity to

00:05:29.750 --> 00:05:34.020
wring a bit of drama out of his departure,
the show's writers opted to have Kutner commit

00:05:34.020 --> 00:05:37.920
suicide. Chilling as that end may have been,
it seems that there were no hard feelings

00:05:37.920 --> 00:05:42.180
between the actor and the showrunners. Penn
came back to reprise his role as Kutner for

00:05:42.180 --> 00:05:45.240
House's series finale in the form of a hallucination.

00:05:45.540 --> 00:05:50.600
The only character to appear in every episode
of the original Dallas, J. R. Ewing was famously

00:05:50.600 --> 00:05:55.650
shot in the cliffhanger episode "A House Divided,"
which left America wondering "Who Shot J.R.?"

00:05:55.650 --> 00:06:00.120
all summer. Though he survived that assassination
attempt, a second shooter finally felled the

00:06:00.120 --> 00:06:05.400
infamous oilman during the second season of
the series' 2010s revival on TNT. While that

00:06:05.400 --> 00:06:08.920
plot twist could have served as a shocking
and clever callback to the original show's

00:06:08.920 --> 00:06:13.740
most famous moment, writers had to include
it out of necessity. Larry Hagman, who played

00:06:13.740 --> 00:06:19.300
J.R. Ewing on both series, lost his battle
to cancer in November 2012. Dallas 2.0 was

00:06:19.300 --> 00:06:23.720
still in production, forcing the creative
staff to both explain his sudden absence while

00:06:23.720 --> 00:06:27.960
also hurriedly writing the character off the
show. The end came with "The Furious and the

00:06:27.960 --> 00:06:32.720
Fast," which aired several months after Hagman's
death, and used pre-existing footage to cobble

00:06:32.720 --> 00:06:34.940
together his character's exit story.

00:06:35.680 --> 00:06:40.520
John Francis Daley was part of the cast of
Fox's long-running procedural drama Bones

00:06:40.520 --> 00:06:46.500
from almost the beginning, but not until the
end. As Dr. Lance Sweets, Daley played a psychologist

00:06:46.500 --> 00:06:51.030
and behavior expert who helped the crime fighters
of the series in their investigations. Over

00:06:51.030 --> 00:06:55.560
the years, Daley developed a second career
as one of the most sought-after comedy screenwriters

00:06:55.560 --> 00:07:00.700
in Hollywood, working on the scripts for Horrible
Bosses, The Incredible Burt Wonderstone, and

00:07:00.700 --> 00:07:05.780
Cloudy with a Chance of Meatballs 2. In 2014,
Daley was offered the chance to not only write

00:07:05.780 --> 00:07:10.740
the new National Lampoon's Vacation, but to
direct it as well. Daley asked for a few months

00:07:10.740 --> 00:07:14.370
off from Bones to do the movie, but the writers
couldn't think of a good way to explain a

00:07:14.370 --> 00:07:18.740
prolonged absence. So his character took a
bullet from a bad guy and died.

00:07:19.260 --> 00:07:23.840
Josh Charles was a huge part of why The Good
Wife turned in such compelling drama each

00:07:23.850 --> 00:07:28.550
week. His character, Will Gardner, was a brilliant
attorney in the show's courtroom scenes, and

00:07:28.550 --> 00:07:32.900
he was also a foil and romantic interest for
Alicia Florrick. That and the fact that The

00:07:32.900 --> 00:07:36.610
Good Wife didn't usually contain a lot of
violence meant it was pretty shocking when

00:07:36.610 --> 00:07:40.220
Will was brutally shot in a courtroom scene
in a 2014 episode.

00:07:41.320 --> 00:07:42.740
"He needs help."

00:07:43.360 --> 00:07:48.280
"Will! Paramedics!"

00:07:48.820 --> 00:07:53.300
But creators Robert and Michelle King didn't
have any animosity toward Charles. Before

00:07:53.300 --> 00:07:57.860
the 2013-14 season began, Charles knew his
contract would be up, and he let the Kings

00:07:57.860 --> 00:08:02.310
know that he wanted to move on, allowing them
to plan an explosive exit for Will. Charles

00:08:02.310 --> 00:08:04.800
explained it all on The Late Show with David
Letterman:

00:08:12.500 --> 00:08:16.760
Character deaths were a semi-regular occurrence
on Scandal. Anyone who got caught up in the

00:08:16.760 --> 00:08:21.090
high crimes and political machinations of
Olivia Pope generally saw their lifespan decrease

00:08:21.090 --> 00:08:25.800
by a few years. That included her fellow fixers
like Harrison Wright, played by Columbus Short.

00:08:25.800 --> 00:08:30.280
Like so many other Scandal standouts, he went
and got himself killed by Olivia's evil father

00:08:30.280 --> 00:08:35.440
Eli Pope, a top-secret CIA operative. At the
end of season three of Scandal, Harrison finds

00:08:35.440 --> 00:08:39.180
out Eli killed the president' son, and it
doesn't end well for Harrison.

00:08:39.180 --> 00:08:44.460
In April 2014, just days after the episode
aired, Short assured viewers that his character

00:08:44.461 --> 00:08:49.620
was really dead: He'd quit the show. After
enduring a series of legal problems, Short

00:08:49.620 --> 00:08:53.540
admitted on Access Hollywood Live that he'd
left Scandal due to a drug issue.

00:08:54.000 --> 00:08:58.360
Even after more than 30 years, Shelley Long
is still best known for her role as Diane

00:08:58.360 --> 00:09:03.800
Chambers on Cheers, a show she famously left
in 1987 for a film career. She never became

00:09:03.800 --> 00:09:08.210
an A-list movie star, but she found one of
her most memorable gigs in the recurring role

00:09:08.210 --> 00:09:12.900
of DeDe Pritchett, the emotionally distant
mother of Claire and Mitchell on Modern Family.

00:09:12.900 --> 00:09:17.540
Long prominently bowed out of this show, too,
although this time it wasn't her idea, the

00:09:17.550 --> 00:09:21.400
Modern Family writers just wanted to explore
the effects of death on the family. Before

00:09:21.400 --> 00:09:26.060
the beginning of Modern Family's tenth season
in 2018, show co-creator Christopher Lloyd

00:09:26.060 --> 00:09:29.660
told Entertainment Weekly that a significant
character on the show would die.

00:09:35.120 --> 00:09:39.140
On the show's Halloween episode, Claire gets
a call from her stepfather in which she learns

00:09:39.140 --> 00:09:43.540
that DeDee Pritchett died in her sleep. The
rest of the episode delves into that impact:

00:09:43.760 --> 00:09:46.120
"Okay, how are we gonna handle this?"

00:09:46.120 --> 00:09:49.560
"You mean because the two most emotionally
complicated people on earth are facing the

00:09:49.560 --> 00:09:51.760
loss of the most complicated relationship
of their lives?"

